#{[17592186400366 :question/problem "Given that the determinant of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 6 & m \\\\ m & 3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis $-29$, find all the possible values of $m.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [17592186400373 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{9}{y}-\\frac{3y}{x}+x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{3}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇g(x,y)=(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $∇g(x,y)=0:$\n\n$$\n(\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1)i-(\\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x})j=0\\,i+0\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}+1=0 \\\\ \\frac{9}{{y}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{{\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ 9\\cdot {\\left(-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}\\right)}^{2}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{3}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{81+3{x}^{3}}{{x}^{4}} & =0 \\\\ 81+3{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-27 \\\\ x & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we get $y=-\\frac{(-3)^{2}}{3}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $∇g(x,y)=0$ at the point: $(-3,-3).$"] [17592186400881 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186400888 :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the squares for the variables ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1})+({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}+4-4)+({x}_{2}^{2}-2{x}_{2}+1-1) & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+({x}_{2}-1)^{2}-4 & =-7 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}({x}_{1}+2)^{2}+({x}_{2}-1)^{2} & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}+2, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}-1,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2} & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{3} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich defines an empty set. Notice that the equation is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(2\\text{i})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3}\\text{i})^{2}}=1,\n$$ which has imaginary solutions, so we can interpret this as an imaginary ellipse with semiaxes $2\\text{i}$ and $\\sqrt{3}\\text{i}.$"] [17592186401034 :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ ?"] [17592186401041 :question/problem "![](images/ca/ca290e8fd51edddf4381ec267b8e0a5692d0e52bd2a36b7d43ab89cbeb5cf00d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?"] [17592186401048 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d683c3960705fa32d11826008a9844c51c8e59e38f5158fce7f4e44c31b97ca9.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?"] [17592186401446 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{16}=1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $y$-axis."] [17592186401453 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{4}=-1,\n$$ find its $z$-intercepts."] [17592186401474 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{2}=1?\n$$"] [17592186401488 :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1?\n$$"] [17592186401558 :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{20}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes."] [17592186401565 :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ intercepts the $x$-axis."] [17592186401572 :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9}=1\n$$ intercepts the $x$-axis."] [17592186401866 :question/problem "![](images/4a/4aa2bca8faab077c47fb968882579233babe7d323ba3120088e8ea5599fbd570.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$$\n\\displaystyle x={e}^{t},\\,y=t\n$$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$ (shown above)?"] [17592186401887 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186402067 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186402074 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-1\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186402375 :question/worked-solution "If $x=0$ is a regular singular point of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation, then the method of Frobenius states that there is at least one solution of the form\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda $ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,…$ are constants.\n\nSubstituting this solution and its derivatives into the ODE, collecting like terms, and setting coefficients equal to zero, we obtain: \n\n- the indicial equation from the coefficients of the $n=0$ term for arbitrary ${a}_{0},$ and\n\n- a recurrence formula from the coefficients of the $n\\ge 1$ terms.\n\n**Step 1**: Write the solution $y$ as a Frobenius power series:\n\n$$\ny={x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the derivatives ${y}^{′}$ and ${y}^{″}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1}, \\\\ {y}^{″} & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Rewrite all the series so that they use the same index of summation.\n\nThe terms of the differential equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =-7{x}^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n-1)(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-2} \\\\  & =-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }7((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x{y}^{′} & =2x\\cdot {\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ (x-5)y & =(x-5){\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n+1}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the first sum of the $y$ term, we change the index of the summation by writing $m=n+1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)y={\\sum }_{m=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{m-1}{x}^{\\lambda +m}-{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nThen, replacing $m$ with $n$ (since it does not matter which letter we use) and isolating the $n=0$ term gives\n\n$$\n(x-5)y={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n-1}{x}^{\\lambda +n}-5{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, isolating the $n=0$ terms for the other terms in the ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7{x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =-7({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }7((\\lambda +n)^{2}-(\\lambda +n)){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}, \\\\ 2x{y}^{′} & =2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2(\\lambda +n){a}_{n}{x}^{\\lambda +n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Consolidate all the series into a single series that is equal to $0.$\n\nCollecting the terms for $n=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda ){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+2\\lambda {a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }-5{a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } &= (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda } \\\\ &= (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+9\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, collecting terms for $n\\ge 1,$ we obtain an infinite series of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n},\n$$\nfor some ${b}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ $n\\ge 1.$\nTherefore, substituting into the homogeneous ODE, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}{x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0 \\\\ (-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(\\lambda ){x}^{n} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**:  Set the coefficients of the series equal to $0.$\n\nSetting the coefficient of the $n=0$ term equal to $0$ with arbitrary ${a}_{0}\\ne 0,$ we obtain the indicial equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-7{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -5){a}_{0} & =0 \\\\ 7{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186402382 :question/worked-solution "The general solution near a regular singular point $x=0$ of a second-order homogeneous linear differential equation with indicial roots ${\\lambda }_{1}\\ge {\\lambda }_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {y}_{1}(x)={x}^{{\\lambda }_{1}}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1}){x}^{n}\n$$ is the first Frobenius solution corresponding to the larger root of the indicial equation, and\n\n- ${a}_{n}({\\lambda }_{1})$ are the coefficients produced by the method when $\\lambda ={\\lambda }_{1}.$\n\nWe are given that the indicial equation is $16{\\lambda }^{2}-21\\lambda +5=0.$\nSolving for the roots, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{\\lambda }^{2}-21\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(16\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =\\frac{5}{16},1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe larger solution is ${\\lambda }_{1}=1.$ Therefore, the first Frobenius series solution we take is\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={x}^{1}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}=x{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}{x}^{n}.\n$$"] [17592186402441 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{27}.\n$$"] [17592186402475 :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic paraboloid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=z+2\n$$ and the plane \n$y=3$  onto the $xz$-plane is a parabola. What is the vertex of this parabola?"] [17592186402502 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle E=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 8 \\\\ -2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186402509 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle P=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186402555 :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$"] [17592186402709 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}6x-4y \\\\ 2x-y \\\\ -5x+3y\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vectors \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$"] [17592186402723 :question/problem "Which of the following statements is true regarding the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }0.12{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}^{3n}\n$$ ?"] [17592186402854 :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+9y &=\\,0 \\\\ \\,\\,\\,x+2y &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the unknown $x$ can be written as  $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{|\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|},\n$$ where ${D}_{x}=$"] [17592186403426 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ and the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-t \\\\ y &=\\sqrt{2}t, \\\\ z &=t+2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186403433 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-2)^{2}+(y+6)^{2}+{z}^{2}=121$ and the line $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-2}{2}=\\frac{y+6}{6}=\\frac{z+11}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186403440 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid ${x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1$ and the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=1+t \\\\ y &=t, \\\\ z &=-t\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186403447 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{15}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{5}=1\n$$ and the line $\\frac{x}{6}=\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{2}.$"] [17592186403454 :question/problem "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1\n$$ and the line $x=\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{-1}.$"] [17592186403475 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z,\n$$ find the points $(x,y,z)$ where $∇f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked."] [17592186403475 :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y}+0-\\frac{1}{z{x}^{2}}-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}+0-0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{1}{xy}+\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{zx}-z) \\\\  & =0+0-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}x}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $$\n\\displaystyle ∇f(x,y,z)=-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k.\n$$ \n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y,z)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}\\,i-\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}\\,k & =0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j+0\\text{ }k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficient of $i$, $j$, and $k$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{cases}-\\frac{z+y}{{x}^{2}yz}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+x}{x{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x}=0\\end{cases}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=-1.$ Substituting these into the third equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1+{z}^{2}x}{{z}^{2}x} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1+{z}^{2}(-1)}{{z}^{2}(-1)} & =0 \\\\ 1-{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-1$ into the first equation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{z+y}{(-1)^{2}yz} & =0 \\\\ z+y & =0 \\\\ y & =-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $z=1,$ we have $y=-1$ and when $z=-1,$ we have $y=1.$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y,z)=0$ at two points: $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(-1,1,-1).$"] [17592186403613 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [17592186403710 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3x+{y}^{2}+2y) \\\\  & =2y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨3{x}^{2}-3,\\,2y+2,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nNote that the tangent plane is horizontal if and only if the normal vector is vertical. This means that the $x$- and $y$-components of the vector must be $0:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3 &=0 \\\\ 2y+2 &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=\\pm 1$ while the second equation gives $y=-1.$ \n\nFinally, substituting $x=-1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(-1)^{3}-3(-1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=1$ and $y=-1$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\nz=(1)^{3}-3(1)+(-1)^{2}+2(-1)=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the points where the tangent plane is horizontal are $(-1,-1,1)$ and $(1,-1,-3).$"] [17592186403724 :question/worked-solution "The Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the surface $z=f(x,y)$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ on the surface can be written as\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=(x-{x}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0})+(y-{y}_{0})\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle ⟨\\frac{∂f}{∂x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),\\frac{∂f}{∂y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0}),-1⟩\n$$ represents a normal vector to the tangent plane.\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x{e}^{y}+2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the normal vector to the tangent plane is \n\n$$\n⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩\n$$\n\nOur tangent plane should be perpendicular to $⟨-2,4,2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of that plane must be parallel to that vector. In other words, for some constant $c$, we must have\n\n$$\nc\\cdot ⟨{e}^{y},\\,x{e}^{y}+2y,\\,-1⟩=⟨-2,4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding coordinates, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}c{e}^{y} &=-2 \\\\ cx{e}^{y}+2cy &=4 \\\\ -c &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $c=-2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-2{e}^{y}=-2\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nAnd substituting $c=-2$ and $y=0$ into the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n-2x{e}^{0}+2(-2)(0)=4\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=-2$ and $y=0$ into our function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =x{e}^{y}+{y}^{2} \\\\  & =(-2){e}^{0}+{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent plane is perpendicular to the vector $v$ at $(-2,0,-2).$"] [17592186403829 :question/problem "Let $f(t)=t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}.$ Find ${g}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)$ given the function $g$ is defined by $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$ for $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"] [17592186403843 :question/problem "Calculate the points where the ellipse \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{6}=1\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis."] [17592186404107 :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ \nfind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$"] [17592186404128 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186404177 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation\n$T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x+4y \\\\ y+z \\\\ x-4z\\end{bmatrix}$ and the vectors \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Which of the given vectors belong to $\\text{Ker}(T)?$"] [17592186404364 :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4y,\\,-4x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-4y &=0 \\\\ -4x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$ | $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$ | $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$ | $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-8$ | $10$ | $-8$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the points $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\n$$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $$\n\\displaystyle (-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}).\n$$"] [17592186404378 :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle O=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186404815 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{\\sqrt{25{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186405029 :question/problem "Which of the following gives $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ with the arc-length integrand simplified pointwise in the given polar angle $\\theta$ (on each smooth piece, without reparametrizing), where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi $ ?"] [17592186405132 :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $$\n\\displaystyle {x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1\n$$ at the point $(0,0,2),$ with parameter $t=0$ at this point."] [17592186405132 :question/worked-solution "First, note that we can write the equation of our surface as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, if we set $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1,\n$$ our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}-1)k \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{z}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,2) & =2(0)\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{9}\\text{ }j+\\frac{(2)}{2}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =k \\\\  & =⟨0,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, the vector form of the equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,0,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,0,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,0,2)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] [17592186405221 :question/worked-solution "Use x=7cosθ, y=7sinθ, 0≤θ≤π/2; scalar arc length is orientation independent. Then $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\int_0^{\\pi/2}294\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta\\,(7)d\\theta=2058/2=1029.\n$$"] [17592186405231 :question/problem "Source question: 8.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C[3y^2dx+3z^2dy+3x^2dz],\n$$ where C is the triangle with vertices (6,0,0), (0,6,0), (0,0,6), oriented counterclockwise. Interpret counterclockwise as viewed from above (positive z).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405233 :question/problem "Source question: 2.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C(22y-x^4)\\,ds\n$$ for $x=t,\\ y=6t,\\ 0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405233 :question/worked-solution "The speed is $\\sqrt{1+36}=\\sqrt{37}$. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\sqrt{37}\\int_0^1(132t-t^4)dt=\\sqrt{37}(66-1/5).\n$$"] [17592186405250 :question/problem "Source question: 8.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and let S bound a solid E satisfying the divergence theorem, with outward normal n. Use $$\n\\displaystyle V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS\n$$ to find the volume of a rectangular parallelepiped with side lengths a,b,c.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405257 :question/worked-solution "$ds=5|\\sec t|\\,|dt|$. On the interval $0\\le t\\le\\pi/3$, with increasing $t$, this is $ds=5\\sec t\\,dt$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle |d\\mathbf r|=\\sqrt{3^2+4^2+25\\tan^2t}\\,|dt|=5\\sqrt{1+\\tan^2t}\\,|dt|=5|\\sec t|\\,|dt|.\n$$ The absolute values express the nonnegative length element."] [17592186405261 :question/worked-solution "The derivatives are $x'=\\cos t-t\\sin t$ and $y'=\\sin t+t\\cos t$, so the squared speed is $1+t^2$. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle s=\\int_0^{5\\pi}\\sqrt{1+t^2}\\,dt=\\frac12[5\\pi\\sqrt{1+25\\pi^2}+\\ln(5\\pi+\\sqrt{1+25\\pi^2})].\n$$"] [17592186405262 :question/worked-solution "The intersections solve x²−x−42=(x−7)(x+6)=0. Use $$\n\\displaystyle A=-\\oint_Cy\\,dx\n$$ with positive orientation: lower parabola from −6 to 7, upper line from 7 to −6. Then $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\int_{-6}^7(x+42-x^2)dx=2197/6.\n$$"] [17592186405263 :question/problem "Source question: 11.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_R16x\\cos(15xy)\\,dA\n$$ over the region bounded by $xy=1$, $xy=5$, $x=1$, $x=16$. Use $u=x$, $v=xy$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405263 :question/worked-solution "Invert: x=u,y=v/u. Since u>0, $|J|=1/u$. The rectangle is 1≤u≤16, 1≤v≤5. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int_1^{16}\\int_1^5 16\\cos(15v)dvdu=16(\\sin75-\\sin15).\n$$"] [17592186405303 :multistep/context "Source question: 13(a)–(c).\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C e^{2y}\\cos x\\,dx+2e^{2y}\\sin x\\,dy\n$$ for each curve C."] [17592186405306 :question/worked-solution "The region is the first-quadrant portion of $r\\le2$ with $x\\ge1$. Thus $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/3$ and $\\sec\\theta\\le r\\le2$. Including the Jacobian, the integral is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/3}\\int_{\\sec\\theta}^2 r\\sqrt{r^2-1}\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\frac13\\int_0^{\\pi/3}(3\\sqrt3-\\tan^3\\theta)\\,d\\theta.\n$$ Since $$\n\\displaystyle \\int\\tan^3\\theta\\,d\\theta=\\tfrac12\\tan^2\\theta+\\ln|\\cos\\theta|,\n$$ its value is $\\pi\\sqrt3/3-1/2+\\ln2/3$."] [17592186405319 :question/problem "Source question: 1.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r.\n$$ The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle6x-3y,20y-3x\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=3x^2-3xy+10y^2$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(3,-1)$ to $(5,4)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}."] [17592186405322 :question/problem "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_S(x+y+z)dS\n$$ on $z=x-20y$ above the xy-region bounded by y=0, x=1, and x=20y.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405331 :question/worked-solution "Use (r cosθ,r sinθ,r), 2≤r≤5. The surface element is √2 r drdθ. Therefore $$\n\\displaystyle M=2\\pi\\sqrt2\\int_2^5(30-r)rdr=2\\pi\\sqrt2[15r^2-r^3/3]_2^5=552\\pi\\sqrt2.\n$$"] [17592186405346 :question/worked-solution "Choose P=−y,Q=0, giving $Q_x-P_y=1$ and $$\n\\displaystyle A=-\\oint_Cy\\,dx\n$$ for positive orientation. Only the upper boundary contributes; it is traversed from x=18 to 10. Hence $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\int_{10}^{18}(x-7)^{-1/2}dx=2(\\sqrt{11}-\\sqrt3).\n$$"] [17592186405357 :question/problem "Source question: 1.\n\nLet $S:z=4x+3y+5$, $0\\le x\\le1$, $0\\le y\\le4$. Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_S(x^2+y^2)dS.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405359 :question/problem "Source question: 2.\n\nLet $S:z=7x^2+6y$, $0\\le x\\le1$, $0\\le y\\le2$. Find $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_S4x\\,dS.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405359 :question/worked-solution "$dS=\\sqrt{37+196x^2}dxdy$. Integrating out y gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 8x\\sqrt{37+196x^2}dx.\n$$ Substitute u=37+196x²; the result is $2[u^{3/2}]_{37}^{233}/147$."] [17592186405380 :question/problem "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C7y\\,ds\n$$ along $y=x^3/2$ from (0,0) to (2,4).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405397 :question/problem "Source question: 10.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and use $$\n\\displaystyle V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS,\n$$ with outward n, to find the volume of a sphere of radius R.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405398 :question/problem "Source question: 9.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y,z\\rangle$ and use $$\n\\displaystyle V=\\frac13\\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS,\n$$ with outward n, to find the volume of a right circular cone of height h and base radius R. Hint: orient the cone with vertex at the origin as shown.\n\n![Cone with vertex at the origin](images/b6/b680c204ce20d30c0451dede0c53b8fbef270bbacb3a2bcc6084796cb91a3018.png)\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405410 :question/problem "Source question: 5.\n\nFor the conservative field $\\mathbf F=\\langle7x^2+y^2,2xy+\\sin y\\rangle$, evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C(7x^2+y^2)dx+(2xy+\\sin y)dy\n$$ from (0,0) to (4,0) along a piecewise smooth curve.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}."] [17592186405428 :question/problem "**(b)** Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C3x\\,dx+8y\\,dy\n$$ along a piecewise smooth curve from (1,3) to (2,5). Integral: {{integral}}."] [17592186405439 :question/problem "Source question: 3.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C3x\\,ds\n$$ for $x=3t,\\ y=7t^2,\\ 0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405473 :question/problem "Source question: 6.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_S(\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F)\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS,\n$$ where $\\mathbf F=\\langle3z,8x,9y\\rangle$ and S is the paraboloid cap $z=6-x^2-y^2$, z≥3. **Source ambiguity:** No normal direction is given; use the upward normal for this study answer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405494 :question/problem "Source question: 11.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F=\\langle x+y,3x-z,y\\rangle$ and let S be $z+2x+2y=4$ in the first octant. Find its outward flux $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS.\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405507 :question/problem "**Question 5 — Part A**\n\nDifferentiate $c=f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $x$, using the chain rule on the right. Conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=-\\dfrac{f_x}{f_z}\n$$ and, similarly, $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=-\\dfrac{f_y}{f_z}.\n$$"] [17592186405511 :question/problem "**Question 2 — Part B**\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y'(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\quad y(0)=4.\n$$\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}, for $x\\ge0$."] [17592186405527 :question/problem "Source question: 7.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_R5x\\,dA,\n$$ where R is enclosed by $5x-y=0$, $5x-y=8$, $x+y=0$, $x+y=3$. Use $u=5x-y$, $v=x+y$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405527 :question/worked-solution "Invert: $x=(u+v)/6$, $y=(5v-u)/6$. The new rectangle is 0≤u≤8, 0≤v≤3, and $|J|=1/6$. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\frac5{36}\\int_0^8\\int_0^3(u+v)dvdu=\\frac5{36}(132)=55/3.\n$$"] [17592186405550 :question/problem "Source question: 2.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r.\n$$ The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle13y^2+26x,26xy\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=13xy^2+13x^2$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(2,2)$ to $(-3,8)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}."] [17592186405566 :question/problem "Source question: 1.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C21x^2y\\,ds\n$$ for $x=\\cos(13t),\\ y=\\cos(13t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/26$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405566 :question/worked-solution "$ds=13\\sqrt2\\sin(13t)dt$ on this interval. With u=cos(13t), the integral is $$\n\\displaystyle 21\\sqrt2\\int_0^1u^3du=21\\sqrt2/4.\n$$ The straight segment from (1,1) to (0,0) gives the same result."] [17592186405569 :question/problem "Source question: 5.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C10x\\,ds\n$$ along $x=3y^3$ from (0,0) to (3,1).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405571 :question/problem "Source question: 7.\n\nFor $f(x,y)=2xe^y$, find its line integrals with respect to x and y along $5x-y=8$ from (0,−8) to (1,−3).\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C f\\,dx=\n$$ {{dx}}.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C f\\,dy=\n$$ {{dy}}."] [17592186405571 :question/worked-solution "Use y=5x−8 and dy=5dx, x from 0 to 1. Integration by parts gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 2xe^{5x-8}dx=[(2/25)e^{5x-8}(5x-1)]_0^1.\n$$ The dy integral is five times the dx integral."] [17592186405578 :question/problem "Source question: 4.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r.\n$$ The field $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{5x}{5x^2+3y^2},\\frac{3y}{5x^2+3y^2}\\right\\rangle\n$$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=\\ln\\sqrt{5x^2+3y^2}$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(3,4)$ to $(5,12)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}."] [17592186405583 :question/worked-solution "Normalize first: $y\\prime-(9/x)y=-6$. Then $$\n\\displaystyle \\alpha=e^{\\int-9/x\\,dx}=x^{-9}\n$$ on the positive interval containing $x=1$. Any nonzero constant multiple is also an integrating factor."] [17592186405599 :multistep/context "Source question: 4.\n\n**The 2D gradient is orthogonal to a level curve**\n\nLet $\\varphi(x,y)$ be a scalar function and consider $\\varphi(x,y)=c$. From Problem 3, $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\\dfrac{\\varphi_x}{\\varphi_y}.\n$$"] [17592186405600 :question/worked-solution "The force is $F=-k r\\prime$, so $$\n\\displaystyle W=-k\\int_0^7\\|r\\prime\\|^2dt=-k\\int_0^7[256+(14-6t)^2]dt=-k(1792+1372).\n$$ The negative sign agrees with resistance to motion."] [17592186405601 :question/problem "Source question: 14.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r\n$$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle x^2y,xy^2\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2t,t^2\\rangle$, $1\\le t\\le2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405613 :question/worked-solution "Divergence is 5+6y+18z. The y term integrates to zero over each centered disk. The area is 9π, so the integral is $$\n\\displaystyle 9\\pi\\int_0^5(5+18z)dz=9\\pi(25+225)=2250\\pi.\n$$ The boundary includes both end disks."] [17592186405620 :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise loop runs from t=0 to t→∞. Green’s area formula gives $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\frac12\\int_0^\\infty(xy′-yx′)dt=32\\int_0^\\infty t^2/(1+t^3)^2dt.\n$$ With u=1+t³ this is $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{32}{3}\\int_1^\\infty u^{-2}du=32/3.\n$$"] [17592186405623 :question/problem "Source question: 3.\n\nUse the **Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals** to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r.\n$$ The field $\\mathbf F=\\langle14xe^{7x^2+2y^2},4ye^{7x^2+2y^2}\\rangle$ is conservative with the supplied potential $f=e^{7x^2+2y^2}$. The piecewise smooth curve joins $(0,0)$ to $(1,1)$.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}."] [17592186405636 :question/problem "Source question: 4.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_Sx^2z\\,dS\n$$ on the cylindrical surface $x^2+y^2=64$, $0\\le z\\le5$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405636 :question/worked-solution "Parameterize (8cosθ,8sinθ,z); the surface element is 8dθ dz. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle I=512\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\cos^2\\theta d\\theta\\int_0^5z\\,dz=512\\pi(25/2)=6400\\pi.\n$$ S contains only the side, not end disks."] [17592186405638 :multistep/context "Source question: 4(a)–(b), PDF page 5.\n\n**Unit vectors in polar coordinates.** Consider $P(x,y)$, $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf r=\\overrightarrow{OP}=\\langle x,y\\rangle=\\langle r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta\\rangle,\n$$ the unit circle centered at P, and\n\n$$\\mathbf i=\\langle1,0\\rangle,\\quad\\mathbf j=\\langle0,1\\rangle,\\quad\\hat{\\mathbf r}=\\langle\\cos\\theta,\\sin\\theta\\rangle,\\quad\\hat{\\boldsymbol\\theta}=\\langle-\\sin\\theta,\\cos\\theta\\rangle.$$\n\n![Source polar unit vectors at P](images/35/35a19c56fb5bee3177ff22b713fb8f5b30ea312559b5a1024abcc8360deb2b9e.png)"] [17592186405670 :question/problem "**Question 2 — Part A**\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y'(x)=x^4,\\quad y(0)=5.\n$$\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186405672 :question/problem "Source question: 12.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_S(x+4y)dS\n$$ for $\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle6u\\cos v,u^2+1,4u\\cos v\\rangle$, $0\\le u\\le1$, $0\\le v\\le\\pi/2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405672 :question/worked-solution "$r_u=(6\\cos v,2u,4\\cos v)$ and $r_v=(-6u\\sin v,0,-4u\\sin v)$. Their cross product is $(-8u^2\\sin v,0,12u^2\\sin v)$, with norm $4\\sqrt{13}u^2\\sin v$. The integrand is $6u\\cos v+4u^2+4$. Integrate in v, then u: $$\n\\displaystyle 4\\sqrt{13}\\int_0^1u^2(3u+4u^2+4)du=4\\sqrt{13}(3/4+4/5+4/3).\n$$"] [17592186405682 :question/worked-solution "The return line is traversed with t decreasing from 3 to 0. Green’s theorem gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_R(Q_x-P_y)dA=0,\n$$ since both cross partials vanish. A check is the global potential $5x^2/2+2y^3/3$: its net change around the closed path is zero."] [17592186405690 :question/problem "Source question: 10.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_R10x\\,dA\n$$ over the ellipse $x^2/16+y^2/49\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405694 :question/problem "Source question: 6.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C(2x+7y)\\,ds\n$$ for $x=\\sin(5t),\\ y=3\\sin(5t),\\ 0\\le t\\le\\pi/5$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405694 :question/worked-solution "The speed is $5\\sqrt{10}|\\cos(5t)|$ and the integrand is 23sin(5t). With u=5t, $$\n\\displaystyle I=23\\sqrt{10}\\int_0^\\pi\\sin u|\\cos u|du=23\\sqrt{10}.\n$$ Split at π/2: each half contributes 1/2. The path retraces the segment, so both passes count."] [17592186405703 :question/problem "Source question: 9.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint_E(x+3)\\,dV\n$$ inside $x^2/16+y^2/25+z^2/36=1$. Use $u=x/4$, $v=y/5$, $w=z/6$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405711 :question/problem "**Question 2 — Part D**\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y'(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x},\\quad y(1)=3.\n$$\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}, on $x>0$."] [17592186405717 :multistep/context "Source question: 6.\n\n**The 3D gradient is orthogonal to a level surface**\n\nLet $\\varphi(x,y,z)$ be a scalar function with level surface $\\varphi(x,y,z)=c$. From Problem 5, $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=-\\dfrac{\\varphi_x}{\\varphi_z}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=-\\dfrac{\\varphi_y}{\\varphi_z}.\n$$"] [17592186405727 :question/problem "Source question: 7.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C\\frac{y\\,dx-x\\,dy}{x^2+y^2}\n$$ about $C:(x-8)^2+(y+7)^2=16$. Enter DNE if it does not exist.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405744 :question/problem "Source question: 6.\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(4x^3-2x^2y)dx+9xy^2dy\n$$ counterclockwise around the region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $x=y^2$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405744 :question/worked-solution "$Q_x-P_y=9y^2+2x^2$. Integrate over 0≤x≤1, x²≤y≤√x. Symmetry under swapping x and y makes both second moments equal: $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_Rx^2dA=\\int_0^1(x^{5/2}-x^4)dx=3/35.\n$$ Thus the result is (9+2)3/35."] [17592186405749 :question/problem "Source question: 9.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C6x\\cos y\\,dx-8y\\sin x\\,dy\n$$ along the segments (0,0) → (2,0) → (2,2) → (0,2), in that order.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405749 :question/worked-solution "The bottom segment contributes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^2 6x dx=12.\n$$ The right segment contributes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^2-8y\\sin2\\,dy=-16\\sin2.\n$$ The top, traversed right to left, contributes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_2^0 6x\\cos2\\,dx=-12\\cos2.\n$$ Do not add a fourth segment."] [17592186405755 :multistep/context "Source question: 12(a)–(c).\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C e^x\\cos y\\,dx-e^x\\sin y\\,dy\n$$ for each curve C."] [17592186405764 :question/worked-solution "$$\n\\displaystyle L=\\int_0^{\\pi/3}5\\sec t\\,dt=5[\\ln|\\sec t+\\tan t|]_0^{\\pi/3}=5\\ln(2+\\sqrt3).\n$$ The integrand is positive throughout the interval."] [17592186405774 :question/problem "**(b)** $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf F=\\frac{\\langle x,y\\rangle}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nPolar form: {{polar}}. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot."] [17592186405778 :question/problem "Source question: 6.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x,z\\rangle$ on $S:x^2+y^2+z^2=49$, z≥0, find $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS\n$$ with outer unit normal n.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405778 :question/worked-solution "On the radius-7 sphere, $n=(x,y,z)/7$, so F·n=z²/7. Use z=7cosφ and dS=49sinφ dφdθ, 0≤φ≤π/2: flux $$\n\\displaystyle =343(2\\pi)\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\cos^2\\phi\\sin\\phi d\\phi=686\\pi/3.\n$$"] [17592186405784 :question/problem "Source question: 6.\n\nFor the conservative field $\\mathbf F=\\langle3x-\\cos y,x\\sin y\\rangle$, evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C(3x-\\cos y)dx+x\\sin y\\,dy\n$$ from $(1,\\pi/2)$ to $(2,\\pi)$ along a piecewise smooth curve.\n\nIntegral: {{integral}}."] [17592186405786 :question/worked-solution "Outward relative to the solid beneath the paraboloid and above z=0 is upward: $n\\,dS=(2x,2y,1)dxdy$. The flux density is 2r²+11. Integrating over r≤9 gives $$\n\\displaystyle 2\\pi\\int_0^9(2r^2+11)rdr=\\pi(9^4+11\\cdot9^2)=7452\\pi.\n$$ Multiply by ρ₀; with unit density omit that factor. No density value is supplied in the source."] [17592186405788 :question/worked-solution "Area is height integrated over the base arc: $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\int_C(3-y^2)ds.\n$$ The speed is 11; u=11t gives $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2\\pi}(3-\\cos^2u)du=6\\pi-\\pi.\n$$"] [17592186405815 :question/problem "Source question: 5.\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(5x^4-5y^2)dx+(7y-4xy)dy\n$$ counterclockwise around the region bounded by $y=x^2$ and $y=\\sqrt x$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405815 :question/worked-solution "The curves intersect at x=0,1 and x²≤y≤√x. Since $Q_x-P_y=-4y+10y=6y$, the integral is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1\\int_{x^2}^{\\sqrt x}6y\\,dy\\,dx=3\\int_0^1(x-x^4)dx=9/10.\n$$"] [17592186405829 :question/problem "**(d)** $$\n\\displaystyle \\mathbf F=\\frac{\\langle-y,x\\rangle}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}=\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf i+\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nPolar form: {{polar}}. Explain how the result agrees with the field plot."] [17592186405833 :question/problem "Source question: 8.\n\nFor $f(x,y)=3xe^y$, find its line integrals with respect to x and y along $x=e^{7y}$ from (1,0) to $(e^7,1)$.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C f\\,dx=\n$$ {{dx}}.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C f\\,dy=\n$$ {{dy}}."] [17592186405833 :question/worked-solution "Parameterize by y from 0 to 1. Then f=3e^{8y} and dx=7e^{7y}dy. The dx integral is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 21e^{15y}dy;\n$$ the dy integral is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 3e^{8y}dy.\n$$"] [17592186405836 :question/problem "**Question 3 — Part B**\n\nUse polar coordinates to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2-y^2}\\,dy\\,dx.\n$$\n\nWhat does this say about $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx\n$$? How about $$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx\n$$?\n\nDouble integral: {{field-1}}.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx=\n$$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx=\n$$ {{field-3}}."] [17592186405836 :question/worked-solution "The first quadrant becomes $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$, $r\\ge0$. The double integral is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-r^2}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=(\\pi/2)(1/2)=\\pi/4.\n$$ Because the nonnegative integrand factors as $e^{-x^2}e^{-y^2}$, this equals $I^2$ where $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-x^2}\\,dx>0.\n$$ Hence $I=\\sqrt\\pi/2$. Evenness doubles it on the real line."] [17592186405847 :question/problem "Source question: 7.\n\nUse Stokes’ theorem to find $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C[2x\\,dx+(3x+y)dy+(6x+y+z)dz],\n$$ where $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle7\\cos t,7\\sin t,7\\rangle$, 0≤t≤2π, counterclockwise.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405855 :question/problem "Source question: 12.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_S\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,dS\n$$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle8z,3x,6y\\rangle$ on the hemisphere $z=\\sqrt{1-x^2-y^2}$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405863 :question/problem "Source question: 8.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint_R7(x+y)\\sin(x-y)\\,dA\n$$ on the triangle with vertices (−5,5), (5,5), (0,0). Use $u=x+y$, $v=x-y$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405863 :question/worked-solution "The inverse is $x=(u+v)/2$, $y=(u-v)/2$, so $|J|=1/2$. The transformed vertices are (0,−10),(10,0),(0,0), giving 0≤u≤10 and u−10≤v≤0. Hence $$\n\\displaystyle I=\\frac72\\int_0^{10}u[\\cos(u-10)-1]du=\\frac72[(1-\\cos10)-50].\n$$ Angles are in radians."] [17592186405864 :question/worked-solution "Mass is $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\rho ds.\n$$ Speed is 18, so $$\n\\displaystyle M=18\\int_0^{\\pi/4}[9\\sin2t+9\\cos2t+2]dt=18(9+\\pi/2).\n$$"] [17592186405874 :question/worked-solution "Arc length integrates speed: $$\n\\displaystyle s=\\int_0^2\\sqrt{(21t^2)^2+(16t)^2}\\,dt=\\int_0^2t\\sqrt{441t^2+256}\\,dt,\n$$ since $t\\ge0$. With $u=441t^2+256$, this is $[(441t^2+256)^{3/2}/1323]_0^2=(2020^{3/2}-4096)/1323$."] [17592186405880 :question/problem "Source question: 1.\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C3y\\,dx\n$$ counterclockwise around the rectangle with vertices (0,0), (2,0), (2,6), (0,6).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405880 :question/worked-solution "$P=3y$, Q=0, so $Q_x-P_y=-3$. Green’s theorem gives −3 times the rectangle area 12, or −36. Directly, only the top horizontal side contributes: $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_2^0 18dx=-36.\n$$"] [17592186405884 :question/worked-solution "$F(r)=(7t^2,3t)$, $r\\prime=(1,2t)$. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle W=\\int_0^1(7t^2+6t^2)dt=13/3.\n$$"] [17592186405891 :question/problem "Source question: 3.\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C8y(x^2+y^2)dx-8x(x^2+y^2)dy\n$$ counterclockwise around the unit circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405891 :question/worked-solution "$Q_x-P_y=-32(x^2+y^2)$. Over the unit disk the double integral is $$\n\\displaystyle -32\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^1r^3drd\\theta=-16\\pi.\n$$ On the circle, direct substitution also gives the constant −8 in the θ integrand."] [17592186405901 :question/problem "Source question: 16.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r\n$$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle e^x,e^{-y},e^{2z}\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle5t,t^2,t^3\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405914 :question/worked-solution "Interpret outward relative to the solid inside the bowl, capped at z=49. On the curved surface the outward vector area is $(2x,2y,-1)dxdy$, so F·dS=−(x²+y²)dxdy. Its integral over r≤7 is $$\n\\displaystyle -2\\pi\\int_0^7r^3dr=-2401\\pi/2.\n$$ Multiply by the constant density ρ₀. This is signed outward mass flow; the positive mass entering is 2401πρ₀/2. With unit density, omit ρ₀. The source supplies no density value and does not distinguish signed flow from positive mass."] [17592186405926 :question/problem "Source question: 4.\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C(x^2-13y^3)dx+(x^2+y^2)dy\n$$ counterclockwise around the unit circle $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405926 :question/worked-solution "$Q_x-P_y=2x+39y^2$. The odd term integrates to zero over the disk; $$\n\\displaystyle \\iint y^2dA=\\pi/4,\n$$ yielding 39π/4."] [17592186405938 :question/worked-solution "Let $P(x)$ be any antiderivative of $p(x)$. Then $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{d}{dx}e^{P(x)}=P\\prime(x)e^{P(x)}=p(x)e^{P(x)}=p(x)\\exp(\\int p(x)\\,dx).\n$$\n\nApply the exponential chain rule and then the definition $P\\prime=p$. The additive constant in $P$ only changes a constant multiplicative factor in the exponential; the displayed derivative identity remains valid."] [17592186405940 :question/problem "Source question: 15.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r\n$$ for $\\mathbf F=\\langle16x^2,xy\\rangle$, $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\sin t,-4\\cos t\\rangle$, $0\\le t\\le\\pi$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405952 :question/problem "**Question 2 — Part C**\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle y'(x)=5\\sin(x),\\quad y(0)=10.\n$$\n\n$y(x)=$ {{field-1}}."] [17592186405988 :question/problem "Source question: 2.\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C2x\\,dy\n$$ counterclockwise around the rectangle with vertices (0,0), (6,0), (6,2), (0,2).\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405988 :question/worked-solution "$Q_x-P_y=2$. The enclosed area is 6·2=12, giving 24. The right side contributes $$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^2 12dy=24,\n$$ and the left side contributes zero."] [17592186405989 :question/problem "Source question: 11.\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_C y^3dx-x^3dy,\n$$ where C is the boundary of the region between $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x^2+y^2=1$.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}."] [17592186405989 :question/worked-solution "Using the standard positive boundary orientation (outer circle counterclockwise, inner circle clockwise), $Q_x-P_y=-3r^2$. Thus $$\n\\displaystyle I=-3\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_1^2r^3drd\\theta=-45\\pi/2.\n$$ The source does not explicitly specify orientation; reversing the full boundary changes the sign."] [17592186406655 :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})},\\,$ ${Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})},\\,$ and $\\,{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})}.$ \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=18-18=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=1-1=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."] [17592186406708 :question/problem "Identify the surface $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186406841 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” is true. Since $y=z=0,$ any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=x\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $Tx=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has more than one solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$” is false. Since $Tx=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has more than one solution, there are ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}$ such that  ${u}_{1}\\ne {u}_{2}$ and \n\n$$\nT({u}_{1})=T({u}_{2})=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis means, $T$ is not a bijection and according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$, then $T$ is invertible” is false. Notice that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” only."] [17592186406843 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ is true. Indeed, since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=4$ is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{rank}(T)=4$ only."] [17592186406881 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}=2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is false. For example, the matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ can't be written as a linear combination of \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\nsince all these linear combinations result in matrices with the entry ${a}_{21}=0:$\n\n$$\na\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+b\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}+c\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ 0 & b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$” only."] [17592186406882 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{4}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis true if and only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}={x}_{4}=0.$ So, the set is linearly independent.\n\n- Statement “$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$” is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}=3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-4)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-2)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-5)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$” is true. Indeed, the set is linearly independent and for any matrix in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R),$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}=a\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-b)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+(-c)\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+d\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a linearly independent set in ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”; “$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$ can be expressed as a linear combination of $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is a basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$”."] [17592186406968 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible, and the image of the quadrilateral under the action of $T$ is a line segment."] [17592186407295 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,4]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-4,4]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-3,4]$” only."] [17592186407296 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/1a/1a5369b58b14444c5ea0ce4bb36954d1d0b7964dcef4e95f9cc527694b3a9e4f.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ equal $x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$; $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [17592186407297 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-5,5]\\times [0,3]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[0,2]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(y{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,2]\\times [0,3]$” only."] [17592186407775 :question/worked-solution "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}A+{x}_{2}B+{x}_{3}C=O.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the matrices $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 4\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1} & 0 \\\\ -{x}_{1} & -{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-8{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{2} \\\\ -5{x}_{2} & 4{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}10{x}_{3} & -3{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} & -5{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+10{x}_{3} & 3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} & -{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-8{x}_{2}+10{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -8 & 10 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}/2 \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -9 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd. Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \n\nThe system is now\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the $2$nd equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substitute it into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-4{x}_{3}+5{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{3}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{3}}=\\frac{{x}_{3}}{{x}_{3}}=1.\n$$"] [17592186407920 :question/problem "![](images/df/df3acf049579ec4de948d84d04d4fe3012c2232b9699d0471857227f52c78107.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x={y}^{2},\\,$ $x=8-{y}^{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\,dA=\n$$"] [17592186407921 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy=\n$$"] [17592186407922 :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b03f2ecd03a89d9484e7d28ab4cb34656b288c3a4554c9a4eeacbe9b61f692c.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $y=2-x,$ above the line $y=1,$ as shown in the picture."] [17592186408037 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ defined on a set $S$ to be a valid probability density function, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nIn our case, for the function $f(x)=k{e}^{-8x}$ on the interval $0<x<\\infty ,$ the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }k{e}^{-8x}\\,\\text{d}x={[-\\frac{k}{8}{e}^{-8x}]}_{0}^{\\infty }=\\frac{k}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this result back into the first equation and solving for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{k}{8}=1\\,⟹\\,k=8.\n$$"] [17592186408038 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$” is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 1,$ we have $f(x)=\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{{x}^{8}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\,{|}_{1}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$” and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 1$”; $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1;\n$$ “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."] [17592186408095 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+(y-1)^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1,\n$$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$"] [17592186408131 :question/problem "![](images/41/4121d975786de30f34e493e7ac49e5cdc8e66621e800321008ba4ba501fb818f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=-{x}^{2},$ and $y=-1,$ as shown above, then $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y-3x)\\,dA=\n$$"] [17592186408132 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\\,dx=\n$$"] [17592186409609 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle F(x,y)=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j,\n$$ what is the scalar curl of $F?$"] [17592186409710 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(2,\\sqrt{5}).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(4,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,2\\le x\\le 4,\\\\\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 5\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ ${(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x<4,\\,{y}^{2}<9}$ only."] [17592186409711 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }-1<x<1,\\\\\\,0<y<1+x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [17592186409785 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186409785 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{1} & \\frac{{3}^{1}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{k} & \\frac{{3}^{k}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already know that the statement is true for some value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{k+1} & \\frac{{3}^{k+1}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{3}^{n} & \\frac{{3}^{n}-1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186409786 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{for all }\\,n\\in N\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}={{field-2}}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-3}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{{field-6}}={{field-7}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186409786 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the **base case**, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side.\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$\n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5\\,\\,k & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5(k+1) & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation using the inductive hypothesis and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we're shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0.5n & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nfor all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186410158 :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{36}=1?\n$$"] [17592186410416 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{{x}^{5}}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{6}}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186410425 :question/worked-solution "![](images/78/7895e1fbe54cd3dea65ba1c75d79c6f325f14cd6bc55f9c7ad99299f00b516df.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },\n$$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186410435 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!}\\right)⋯\\left(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}\\right) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!\n$$ is a constant and $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe are told that $$\n\\displaystyle S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=6,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=2,\n$$ and $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,{\\theta }^{6}\\,{e}^{-5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186410546 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}})=\n$$"] [17592186410546 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, our matrix is already in a row echelon form.\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}})=3.\n$$"] [17592186410547 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}})=\n$$"] [17592186410547 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}})=2.\n$$"] [17592186410548 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}}.\n$$"] [17592186410575 :question/worked-solution "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields. \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}$ and $Q=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y$ and $Q=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}+1}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8x-3y$ and $Q=4y-3x+1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-3.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle F=\\frac{2x}{{e}^{y}}\\,i-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{y}}\\,j;\n$$ $H$ $=(8x-3y)\\,i$ $+(4y-3x+1)\\,j$ only."] [17592186411285 :question/problem "Determine if $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?"] [17592186411859 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d83f771aa650879909c33d7033e80ca044b7e6f8369e19667e6173aa8a40737.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+\\sqrt{3y})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(2,0)$ to the point $(0,3),$ as shown above."] [17592186411861 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\,dA,\\,\n$$\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le 2-x\\}.$"] [17592186411863 :question/problem "![](images/55/55bbf6f55ce287321f2cc593ea48d65a78c655ca18d2a34df68b63c2bda92c8f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is the semicircle shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186411874 :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a66f9263c9009f634668e826f0a84320103589b9c270360d7b2465c71e74592.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∭}_{R}2xz\\,dV\n$$\nover the region $R,$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*"] [17592186412421 :question/problem "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [17592186412973 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the residue classes modulo $n$ have the following properties:\n\n- There are exactly $n$ distinct residue classes modulo $n$\n\n- Two distinct residue classes have an empty intersection: $[a]_{n}\\cap [b]_{n}=∅$ if $a≢b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$\n\n- Residue classes modulo $n$ form a partition of the set of all integers: \n\n$$\nZ=[0]_{n}\\cup [1]_{n}\\cup ⋯\\cup [n-1]_{n}\n$$\n\n- The integers modulo $n$ is defined as the set containing all distinct residue classes modulo $n$: \n\n$$\n{Z}_{n}=\\{[0]_{n},\\,[1]_{n},\\,…,\\,[n-1]_{n}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following properties:\n\n1\\. $$\n\\displaystyle {⋃}_{i=0}^{222}\\,[\\,i\\,]_{223}=Z\n$$\n\n2\\. $[\\,21\\,]_{223}\\cap [\\,162\\,]_{223}=∅$\n\n3\\. ${Z}_{223}=\\{[\\,i\\,]_{223}\\,∣\\,0\\le i<223\\}$"] [17592186413042 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(3x+y)\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the parabola parametrized by $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(4t-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2].$"] [17592186413043 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx+{z}^{3}\\,dy-{y}^{2}\\,dz,\n$$ where $C$ is a portion of the line $r(t)=\\sqrt{2}t\\,i+2t\\,j+2t\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,4]?$"] [17592186413190 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =3, & \\,{m}_{2} & =1, & \\,{m}_{3} & =6, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1, & \\,{x}_{2} & =3, & \\,{x}_{3} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+1+6 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+3+24 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{30}{10}=3.\n$$"] [17592186413194 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=4,\\,{m}_{2}=1,\\,{m}_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=2,\\,{y}_{2}=0,\\,{y}_{3}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =4+1+3 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =8+0+12 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{20}{8}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186413196 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =4, & \\,{m}_{2} & =7, & \\,{m}_{3} & =1, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =3, & \\,{x}_{2} & =4, & \\,{x}_{3} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =4+7+1 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 3+7\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =12+28+8 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{48}{12}=4.\n$$"] [17592186413197 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=4,\\,{m}_{2}=3,\\,{m}_{3}=5.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=2,\\,{y}_{2}=-1,\\,{y}_{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =4+3+5 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =8-3+15 \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{20}{12}=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186413198 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=4,\\,{m}_{2}=2,\\,{m}_{3}=2,\\,{m}_{4}=6.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $y$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}=1,\\,{y}_{2}=4,\\,{y}_{3}=1,\\,{y}_{4}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =4+2+2+6 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{y}_{1}+{m}_{2}{y}_{2}+{m}_{3}{y}_{3}+{m}_{4}{y}_{4} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4+8+2+18 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{32}{14}=\\frac{16}{7}.\n$$"] [17592186413201 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =3, & \\,{m}_{2} & =5, & \\,{m}_{3} & =6, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =2, & \\,{x}_{2} & =4, & \\,{x}_{3} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3} \\\\  & =3+5+6 \\\\  & =14\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 4+6\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =6+20+30 \\\\  & =56\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot \\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{56}{14}=4.\n$$"] [17592186413202 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass, denoted $\\overset{―}{x},$ of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ along the $x$-axis is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =3, & \\,{m}_{2} & =2, & \\,{m}_{3} & =5, & \\,{m}_{4} & =6, \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3, & \\,{x}_{2} & =-1.5, & \\,{x}_{3} & =2, & \\,{x}_{4} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =3+2+5+6 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the total moment of the system about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-1.5)+5\\cdot 2+6\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-9-3+10+18 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{16}{16}=1.\n$$"] [17592186413204 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a collection of point masses ${m}_{1},{m}_{2},…,{m}_{n}$ located at $({x}_{1}{y}_{1}),({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),…,({x}_{n},{y}_{n})$ on the plane is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\n \nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}\n$$ is the total mass of the system,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{y}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i}\n$$ is the total moment of the system about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis.\n\nLet's denote the masses as\n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=3,\\,{m}_{2}=6,\\,{m}_{3}=4,\\,{m}_{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThen, the corresponding $x$-coordinates of the masses are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-4,\\,{x}_{2}=-1,\\,{x}_{3}=3,\\,{x}_{4}=4.\n$$\n\nSo, the total mass of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}+{m}_{2}+{m}_{3}+{m}_{4} \\\\  & =3+6+4+2 \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the moment of the system about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{m}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={m}_{1}{x}_{1}+{m}_{2}{x}_{2}+{m}_{3}{x}_{3}+{m}_{4}{x}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-4)+6\\cdot (-1)+4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-12-6+12+8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{2}{15}.\n$$"] [17592186413265 :question/problem "Consider the straight line with the canonical equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-7}{3}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z+3}{-7}.\n$$ Find the vector equation of the line."] [17592186413268 :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the line $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-10}{-6}=\\frac{y+5}{3}=\\frac{z+7}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186413269 :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the line $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x}{7}=\\frac{y-3}{2}=\\frac{z+8}{-6}.\n$$"] [17592186413273 :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the line $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-13}{-7}=\\frac{y+2}{3}=\\frac{z-1}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186413341 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to zero due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-x(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-x) & =(-x)^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-g(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\n- The function $h(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x)^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{1}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [17592186413342 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=p,\n$$ express the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ in terms of $p.$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186413343 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any odd function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nSo, to determine which integrals evaluate to $0$ due to symmetry, we have to determine which integrands are odd functions.\n\n- The function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ is *not* an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & ={e}^{2(-x)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\  & \\ne -f(x)\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is an odd function, but integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt[3]{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is not of the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-a}^{a}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ since the upper and lower limits are not opposites. Therefore, we cannot use symmetry to simplify the integral.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x{e}^{{x}^{2}}$ is an odd function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-x) & =(-x){e}^{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-x{e}^{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-h(x)\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by symmetry, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{4}x{e}^{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ only."] [17592186413344 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ using symmetry.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186413415 :question/problem "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}8xy, & \\,0\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle P(\\frac{1}{4}<X<\\frac{1}{2}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{2}{3}).\n$$"] [17592186413605 :question/worked-solution "A *decoupled system* consists of equations depending on only one variable, such as\n\n$$\n{x}_{i}^{′}(t)={\\lambda }_{i}{x}_{i}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\lambda }_{i}$ is a constant. This means that we can solve each equation independently. \n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our systems.\n\n- System $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+5{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\n- System $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-2{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is *not* decoupled because some of its equations depend on more than one variable. For example, ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on ${x}_{2}(t)$ and ${x}_{1}(t).$\n\n- System $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-7{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=9{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ is decoupled since its equations depend on only one variable each. Indeed, ${x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-7{x}_{1}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}^{′}(t)=9{x}_{2}(t)$ depends on only ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) &=-7{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t) &=9{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only."] [17592186413716 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186413735 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(0,0,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2+2t \\\\ y &=5t \\\\ z &=1-t\\end{aligned}\\right..\n$$"] [17592186413790 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}e & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2} &=5{e}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2} &=3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4e{c}_{2}=8{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{2}=2e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e(2e)=3{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}={e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{-3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{5t}+2e\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186413832 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle v=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2-1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4-1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{w}_{1}+9{w}_{2} &=3 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+3{w}_{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=3{w}_{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=3,b=-1,$ and $a+b=2.$"] [17592186414135 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+4\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+4\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-4\\text{i} & -8 \\\\ 2 & -4\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+4\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(3+4\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(3+4\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}(2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186414136 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 1 & -\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(3+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\ {e}^{3t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}({e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"] [17592186414139 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$$\n\\displaystyle {v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2+3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-2+3\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-2+3\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}(-{e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}({e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186414540 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,+\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}t{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186414544 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\,+\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}+{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186414750 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 3}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{7} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{14} \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{14}}{14}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\,0\\,|+|\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{10}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}|=\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186414751 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-4.\n$$"] [17592186414752 :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2}{(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=0,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$"] [17592186414753 :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B={{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{17}{6} \\\\ \\frac{11}{6} \\\\ \\frac{7}{3}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=\\frac{17}{6},$ $b=\\frac{11}{6},$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{17}{6}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{11}{6}\\right)}=\\frac{17}{11}.\n$$"] [17592186414783 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works against the particle for $x>0,$ while $F$  works with the particle for $x<0.$ \n\n![](images/af/af80eab6427f376084301c03715fd098cfe4a1c816dcd72d3e1129cfa35ff29d.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/a2/a26ca79400bf30aa891543d30a052a6e8ec071ccb48fcd8c418fe0a713dad212.png)\n\n \n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ only."] [17592186414784 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9805268d9f4984983c3cc5e2200a0c22d804bc67076a3505ad35471693c037b.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/da/dafb4d468e7711a804a5141301a02331bb5c7900f018a8ecdb6fd382bc05b799.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [17592186414785 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/87/8726ab8c3d764016e1bfa5206769674b6809ab3ea047b03734ae5141f0b553b9.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/56/56e77c37324ca2f28c0a09d762d1e5e1f4a29f5f00695e5cbadca748d92b1c6f.png)\n\n \nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [17592186414786 :question/worked-solution "Remember that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is false while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/e7/e783fb958f01da9552ef4258adf4bed1f7b083520ad9a59082b5479dc2fc7d40.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/20/20798c6992f536d4f280243746f8599bd4589bd69e253caeed65150b8a9944b7.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ only."] [17592186414917 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=-x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is a circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction."] [17592186414918 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C$ is the arc of a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin traversed counterclockwise from the point $(0,3)$ to the point $(-3,0).$"] [17592186414919 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=4(x+y)\\,i-3x\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(2,1)$ to the point $B(3,3).$"] [17592186414920 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(y-x)^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-1,-1)$ to the point $B(-2,0).$"] [17592186414921 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{x}\\,i+{y}^{6}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-1,1)$ to the point $B(1,2)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-1+2t)\\,i+(1+t)\\,j.$*"] [17592186414922 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)^{2}\\,i+{e}^{x-y}\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-2,1)$ to the point $B(1,-1)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-2+3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j.$*"] [17592186414923 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=\\sqrt{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=x-2$ traversed from the point $A(2,1)$ to the point $B(4,{e}^{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2)\\,i+t\\,j.$*"] [17592186414924 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+9}$ traversed from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(4,5).$"] [17592186414925 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(2,{e}^{2}).$"] [17592186414926 :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb8c2591ed5068812fa1d184d4a93a6c71b0d2ef77bb01debd4b2a03211742f7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F(r)\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+5y\\,j$ and $C$ is the the arc of the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $4$ respectively, traversed anticlockwise from $A(2,0)$ to $B(0,4).$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.$*"] [17592186414927 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,j$ and $C$ is the curve $y={e}^{2x}$ traversed from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,{e}^{2})?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\,j.$*"] [17592186414928 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the line segment traversed from the point $A(-1,-2)$ to the point $B(3,4)?$\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=(-1+4t)\\,i+(-2+6t)\\,j.$*"] [17592186415103 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=y{e}^{xz}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1,{t}^{2}-1,{t}^{2}-2t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186415104 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y+{x}^{2}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186415105 :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t,\\frac{\\pi t}{2}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for $F=(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y)\\,i-({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*"] [17592186415106 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{4},{t}^{6}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186415107 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$"] [17592186415143 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 \\\\ -18 & 54\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -3 \\\\ -18 & 54 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\n Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\frac{3}{1}=2.\n$$"] [17592186415272 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =e\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2e-1,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\sqrt{x},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=e,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” only."] [17592186415273 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(2,0)$ to $(3,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186415274 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+2y\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-1,1)$ to $(\\sqrt{3},2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186415275 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2},\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=xy.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2},2)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-\\sqrt{2},\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{2},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2\\sqrt{2},\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{OA},$ traversed from $O$ to $A.$” only."] [17592186415276 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=6xy\\,i+3{x}^{2}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0)$ to $(2,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186415277 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,j.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0)$ to $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186415278 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x+y}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-2,3,1)$ to $(0,1,-1)$ in ${R}^{3}.$"] [17592186415279 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡z-y.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(1,0,1)$ to $(2,-2,0)$ in ${R}^{3}.$"] [17592186415280 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=y{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(0,2)$ to $(\\pi ,1)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186415281 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is true. Notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives exist and are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is true. Since $F$ is conservative, there exists a function $f$ such that $∇f=F.$ Reconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf=x{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-1\\cdot {e}^{0} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=3,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is false. Since $F$ is conservative and ${C}_{2}$ has the same initial and terminal points as ${C}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$F$ is conservative”; “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=8,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” only."] [17592186415312 :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$ is true, while statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/b2/b2473eb0c505d75d2c448c1a0d714d05d42737ce8d601401e925834a25ae9e14.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/85/8513d9d15d204b6f63f310a37465d6b8dfb21239ca412bac7d3b9936f2bf5ebf.png)\n\nSo, $$\n\\displaystyle Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0;\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0\n$$ only."] [17592186415314 :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4x}{1+4{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$F$ is **not** conservative” is false. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is false, while statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$” is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$” only."] [17592186415439 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx+x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$"] [17592186415440 :question/problem "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y\\,dx-x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(\\sqrt{2},0)$ to the point $B(0,\\sqrt{2}).$"] [17592186415441 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi y\\,dx-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,dy=\n$$"] [17592186415442 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y={x}^{2}-1$ traversed from the point $P(1,0)$ to point $Q(2,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,dx+x\\,dy=\n$$"] [17592186415443 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{FG}$ traversed from the point $F(1,0)$ to the point $G(2,3),$ then $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x\\,dx-xy\\,dy=\n$$"] [17592186415537 :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right),$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,-\\pi \\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right),$ we obtain $$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(y)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right).\n$$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)\\sqrt{1+{(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{y}{2}\\right)}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186415676 :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbc6ddf87d5f5502db656242222dca27fb33ded5d3bdcf372b70ef10eda07624.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a three-quarter circle of radius $3,$ traversed from the point $(-1,-3)$ to the point $(-4,0),$ shown above."] [17592186415677 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=17,\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=7,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=5,\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\n$$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}.$"] [17592186415677 :question/worked-solution "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}\\cup {C}_{3}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 17 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+5-{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 17 & =-7+5-{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 19 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-19.\n$$"] [17592186415678 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=14\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-6,\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\n$$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [17592186415678 :question/worked-solution "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =14-(-6) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186415679 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d1c20f44c7a092275f200737813e11c762e1f3e80ba22ded29b41731e22472e.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is the path comprising of two line segments, as shown above."] [17592186415680 :question/problem "![](images/71/71620d1e3e6691ef6c91382665fd258891984b1e32aeaec122663d65c1ca5b1b.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment between the points $(-3,-1)$ to the point $(1,3),$ as shown above."] [17592186415681 :question/problem "![](images/05/05b76357203cf2d7ef38b4bfa8daa9e0ce21b248f150e1a2d516f1a4c4e5bef3.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter circle of radius $1,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(1,1),$ shown above."] [17592186415682 :question/problem "![](images/77/77c069c4b4306b3337a44ddfc53ecd4d727e78da09d77d61e84fc01d3b30c302.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed path along the square of \nside $2,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above."] [17592186415770 :question/problem "![](images/87/87182d625f4e669a6b2791a17f3e12c34922043b8f3deefd6b2473e8fb529886.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=4-{x}^{2}$ from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [17592186415771 :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c3b1312e7368d8fc760a274a49280b10d4571a06ee6237b28fc8a8b1b7316d3.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $DEFD,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?"] [17592186415772 :question/problem "![](images/74/746a05b2345e50173ed898b741944ba435d17a44dfc075848be9d8fcd2f2ad90.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $PQRP,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?"] [17592186415773 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(y-z)\\,\\text{d}x+6{z}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-4y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$"] [17592186415774 :question/problem "![](images/b4/b4ce481ac8babb7a3e5707f1ef44417b12e73abe4b4c2143c19b45332642c6d2.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment  from the point $B(0,8)$ to the point $A(-2,8),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a section of the curve $y=-{x}^{3}$ from the point $A(-2,8)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=16,\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=xy\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [17592186415775 :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)={e}^{y}\\,i+{e}^{x}\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [17592186415860 :question/problem "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-1,0),(1,0)$ and $(-1,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({y}^{2}-2xy)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2})\\,dy\n$$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186415861 :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the $y$-axis, for $x\\ge 0.$ Express $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}(x{y}^{2}-{e}^{x})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-1)\\,dy\n$$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186415863 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}(2x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dx+(x+{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the boundary of the trapezoid with vertices at $(0,1),$ $(0,-5),$ $(4,-1)$ and $(4,1).$"] [17592186415866 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨6{x}^{2}-xy+11,{y}^{2}+1⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(0,0),$ $(2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$"] [17592186415868 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}xy\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}+y)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the boundary of the rectangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(3,0),$ $(3,2)$ and $(-1,2).$"] [17592186415869 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}(2xy-{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2x)\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is the boundary of the triangle with vertices at $(-1,0),$ $(1,0)$ and $(-1,2).$"] [17592186415870 :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(-2,0),(1,0),(1,1)$ and $(-2,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,dx+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186416109 :question/problem "![](images/e5/e54e2dc5c314ed1dda6ef9c60db5c5df3058ba120afec42bddbabc2622c3ae98.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Given that $F(x,y)=⟨x+y,\\,-xy⟩,$ which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr\\,?\n$$"] [17592186416110 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=⟨{sin}^{3}⁡x-{y}^{3},{y}^{4}+{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ oriented counterclockwise."] [17592186416140 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1,\n$$ \nthen what is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}(4-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{6}(4-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186416600 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n$$\n\\displaystyle {P}_{B\\to C}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ."] [17592186416924 :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|\n$$ is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only.\n\n- Expression $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$ is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15},\\sigma )$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|;\n$$ $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$ only."] [17592186416928 :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expressions $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ and $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ are statistics. They are functions of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ only.\n\n- Expression $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{\\left(\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\sigma }{\\mu }\\right)}^{2}\n$$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu $ and the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$; $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$ only."] [17592186416929 :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}\n$$ is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$ is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only.\n\n- Expression $\\frac{1}{3}({X}_{2}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{6})$ is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$; $\\frac{1}{3}({X}_{2}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{6})$ only."] [17592186416930 :question/problem "Suppose the following data are sampled from a population.\n\n$$\n12,\\,14,\\,9,\\,13,\\,12\n$$\n\nCalculate the value of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({X}_{i}-12)^{2}\n$$ for this particular sample."] [17592186416931 :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression $({X}_{1}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{7})^{3}$ is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expressions $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}}{2}\n$$ is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expressions $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{{X}_{i}^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}\n$$ is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $({X}_{1}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{7})^{3}$ only."] [17592186417019 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186417020 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186417021 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186417022 :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186417067 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=6{x}^{2}+1,\\,a=0,\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the mass of the rod is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{2}+1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}+x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+1 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot (6{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{x}^{3}+x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186417068 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a rod placed along the $x$-axis over the interval $a\\le x\\le b$ whose mass density function is $\\lambda (x)$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the mass of the rod,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{O}={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\,x\\lambda (x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$ is the moment of the rod about the origin $O.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}},\\,a=1,\\,b=8.\n$$\n\nSo,  the moment of the rod about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{O} & ={\\int }_{1}^{8}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{8}{x}^{2/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5/3}]}_{1}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(32-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{93}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{O}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{93}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{62}{15}.\n$$"] [17592186417356 :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}3{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ u+2{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186417703 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. Since $T(x)=v$ has no solution, we have that $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ hence $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5}.$ According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{5},$ the transformation $T$ and the matrix $T$ are not invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that  $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ is false. Since $T$ is not invertible, the invertible matrix theorem implies that $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ only."] [17592186417704 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is false. Since $T$ is a linear transformation and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ we have $T({u}_{1}-{u}_{2})=0.$ So ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is invertible” is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3,$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ only."] [17592186418037 :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & ={{field-12}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis true for all {{field-15}}."] [17592186418037 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5(1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5k \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5k+5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5(k+1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5n \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis true for all $n\\in N.$"] [17592186418038 :question/problem "Use mathematical induction to prove that\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nfor all $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is {{field-6}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-7}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-8}}={{field-9}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-10}} & ={{field-11}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & ={{field-12}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={{field-13}} \\\\  & ={{field-14}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & {5}^{n}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis true for all {{field-15}}."] [17592186418038 :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{1} & {5}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$ \n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}=\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{k} & {5}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{k+1} & {5}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving matrix identities using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to factor out one instance of the matrix, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1}={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k+1} & ={\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{k}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{k} & {5}^{k}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5({5}^{k}) & 4({5}^{k})+({5}^{k}-1) \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{k}\\cdot {5}^{1} & {5}^{k}\\cdot {5}^{1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{k+1} & {5}^{k+1}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction,\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{n}=\\begin{bmatrix}{5}^{n} & {5}^{n}-1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nis true for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [17592186418387 :question/problem "Prove that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)\n$$ using mathematical induction.\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=3}^{3}(2i+3)={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich equals the right-hand side. Thus, our statement is {{field-3}} for $n=3.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-4}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3)={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+{{field-6}}.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3 & ={{field-7}} \\\\  & ={{field-8}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)\n$$ is true for all {{field-9}}."] [17592186418387 :question/worked-solution "A variant of the principle of induction states the following: Let ${n}_{0}$ be an integer. Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P({n}_{0}),P({n}_{0}+1),P({n}_{0}+2),…,$ where\n\n- $P({n}_{0})$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every natural number $k\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every natural number $n\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(3).$ To do this, we substitute $n=3$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=3.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=3}^{3}(2i+3)=2(3)+3=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+6)(3-2)=9(1)=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=3.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 3.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=3,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 3,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3)=(k+6)(k-2).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=(k+7)(k-1).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}{a}_{i}=({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}{a}_{i})+{a}_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=3}^{k+1}(2i+3)=({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3.\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=3}^{k}(2i+3))+2(k+1)+3 & =(k+6)(k-2)+(2k+5) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+4k-12+2k+5 \\\\  & ={k}^{2}+6k-7 \\\\  & =(k+7)(k-1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=3}^{n}(2i+3)=(n+6)(n-2)\n$$ is true for all $n\\ge 3.$"] [17592186418388 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to prove the following statement using mathematical induction:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$\n\nA proof template is outlined below. Fill in the missing statements.\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}2(5i-3)={{field-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-2}}, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}2(5i-3)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}2(5i-3)={{field-4}}.\n$$\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [17592186418388 :question/worked-solution "To prove a statement using induction, we use the following framework:\n\n- **Step 1**: Base Case\n\n- **Step 2**: Inductive Hypothesis\n\n- **Step 3**: Inductive Step\n\n- **Step 4**: Conclusion\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called **base case,** $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side. \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}2(5i-3)=1(5-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProving that the base case is true is usually straightforward. We simply calculate the left-hand and right-hand sides independently and show they're equal.\n\nNext, we write down our **inductive hypothesis**. We assume that $P(k)$ is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ To do this, we simply replace $n$ with $k$ in our original statement $P(n).$ \n\nIn our case, this gives the following:\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}2(5i-3)=k(5k-1).\n$$\n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k=1,$ so this assumption is valid. \n\nThen, we move onto the **inductive step**: We must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ We start by writing down $P(k+1).$ \n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}2(5i-3) & =(k+1)(5(k+1)-1) \\\\  & =(k+1)(5k+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inductive step is where most of the work lies. The idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of this equation *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nOnce we've shown that $P(k)⇒P(k+1),$ the proof is complete, and we state our **conclusion**.\n\n**Conclusion**: Therefore, $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}2(5i-3)=n(5n-1)\n$$ for all $n\\ge 1.$"] [17592186418389 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle S(k)={\\sum }_{i=0}^{k}(2-i),\n$$ then $S(k+1)=$"] [17592186418459 :question/worked-solution "If ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2},$ then\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu ,\\,\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n},\n$$\n\nand the standard error is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]}=\\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the bills in the population have denominations $$5$ and $$10,$ and these are distributed in the ratio $3:2.$ Therefore, to compute the population's mean and variance, we can consider a population consisting of $3+2=5$ bills only with the following denominations:\n\n$$\n$5,\\,$5,\\,$5,\\,$10\\,$10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(15+20) \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{5} \\\\  & =$7,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the population variance is\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}^{2}-{\\mu }^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\cdot {5}^{2}+2\\cdot {10}^{2})-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(275)-49 \\\\  & =55-49 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n\n$$$\n\nSince the sample size is $n=4,$ we have\n\n$$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\text{Var}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}=\\frac{6}{4}=1.5.\n\n$$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\sqrt{1.5}≈$1.22.\n$$"] [17592186418766 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vector that spans the subspace $H$ as ${h}_{1}.$ \n\nA vector $x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to ${H}^{⊥}$ if $x\\cdot {h}_{1}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {h}_{1} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which is already in row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $1$ pivot column (the $1$st one), so ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the equation, we get ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{4}(2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}).$\n So, every vector in ${H}^{⊥}$ has the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4}(2{x}_{2}-5{x}_{3}) \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $$\n\\displaystyle B={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$ is a basis of ${H}^{⊥}.$"] [17592186418767 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ⇔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -\\frac{1}{4} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=2.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b+c}{a}=\\frac{2+2}{1}=4.$"] [17592186418769 :question/worked-solution "The vector $u$ is orthogonal to the set $W$ if $u\\cdot {w}_{1}=0$ and $u\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$ So, we need to solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot {w}_{1} &=0 \\\\ u\\cdot {w}_{2} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3} &=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M,$ which we reduce to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -3 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, we have $2$ pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd), so ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3}.$ So, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the vector \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain $a=3,$ $b=2,$ and $c=4.$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{a}{b+c}=\\frac{3}{2+4}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186419017 :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector-valued function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nThis theorem implies that the line integral of a conservative vector field depends only on the value of $f$ at the initial and terminal points $A$ and $B,$ and is independent of the path between them.\n\nWith that in mind, we consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0,\n$$ where ${C}_{1}$ is the path along the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ in counterclockwise direction” is true. Parameterizing the curve, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{1}(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t{|}_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi  \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0,\n$$ where ${C}_{2}$ is the path along the segment line $\\overset{―}{AB},$ traversed from $A$ to $B.$” is true. Parameterizing the line segment, we have\n\n$$\n{r}_{2}(t)=⟨0,\\,1-2t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}⟨2t-1,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is independent of path” is false. Since ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ have the same initial and terminal points, yet\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ is path-dependent.\n\n- Statement “$F$ is conservative” is false. If a vector field is conservative then it is path-independent. Since $F$ is path-dependent, it is not conservative."] [17592186419225 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type I plane region:\n\n![](images/e6/e6027dcb30cf0d638dc4472c40182ddf9bd85d52840d1268d00c4d3ecbc09abc.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{x}}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4/x}y\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}[{\\int }_{0}^{4/x}y\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot {[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4/x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{x}(\\frac{8}{{x}^{2}}-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}]}_{x=1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =-1+4 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"] [17592186419230 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-y}x\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}(2-y)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{10}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-8+\\frac{16}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{15}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$"] [17592186419232 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nNotice that $D$ is a type II plane region:\n\n![](images/e0/e01f73436854c4d337f4fa1dfbb31645c99ccdccbd4e36ae5145305f8b7b4a1f.png)\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le -y,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda (x,y)=x-y.$ \n\nSince we require the $y$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $x$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\cdot (x-y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}xy-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{-y}xy-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}-x{y}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=-y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}\\frac{3{y}^{3}}{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{3{y}^{4}}{8}]}_{y=-1}^{y=0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{8}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186419236 :question/problem "Consider the lamina that occupies the region $$\n\\displaystyle D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi x}{4}}\n$$ and has the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}.$ Given that the mass of the lamina is $\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ units and its center of mass is located at $(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y}),$ what is the value of $\\overset{―}{x}?$"] [17592186419236 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x/4)}x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x/4)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot [\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x/4)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x(\\frac{\\pi x}{4}-0)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{\\pi {x}^{3}}{12}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186419237 :question/worked-solution "The center of mass of a lamina $D$ with the mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(\\overset{―}{x},\\overset{―}{y})=(\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m},\\frac{{M}_{x}}{m}),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle m={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the mass of the lamina,\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{x}={∬}_{D}y\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\\,\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $x$-axis, and\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {M}_{y}={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ is the moment of the lamina about the $y$-axis.\n\nSince we require the $x$-coordinate of the center of mass, we need to compute the moment about the $y$-axis: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}x\\,\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x\\cdot {x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{-y}^{0}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{x=-y}^{x=0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\cdot (0-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{4}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{{y}^{6}}{24}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{{M}_{y}}{m}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{24}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{15}\\right)}=-\\frac{5}{8}.\n$$"] [17592186419421 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(7,4,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z+1}{-2}.\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+4y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$"] [17592186419422 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-1,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-2}{-1}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z+1}{1}.\n$$"] [17592186419579 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2x+4y-2z \\\\ -3x-6y+3z \\\\ 4x+8y-4z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ Find $\\,\\text{Ker}(T).$"] [17592186419581 :question/problem "Given the linear transformation $$\n\\displaystyle T:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}9y-3x+6z \\\\ 3y-x+2z \\\\ 6x-12z-18y\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $\\text{nullity}(T).$"] [17592186419596 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}+(z-1)^{2}=1,\n$$ find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$"] [17592186420019 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1c4133695eeb868f563f80021755fea75bef622e1ad46cbbf6daff1c953cb075.png)"] [17592186420020 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{3t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/e9/e9fa4226361b8c0abe6c5b02374967b025aa8870757150f86b15434841825e2d.png)"] [17592186420021 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ae/ae1135571904a4bfb49eb79e187fa6d1754fc562fe66b604d33eb555418a1345.png)"] [17592186420022 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-6t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/b9/b99c7dc98a7a43dabb0fb7222fd6b4eeaaec727bc180953ee24769389667c501.png)"] [17592186420023 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/36/362ee182f39f9d5cc1e7597f7e836a6ce802b4762a03c347c5701c0830f3d523.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-5t})^{′}}=\\frac{-3{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}}{-5{c}_{1}{e}^{-5t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{5{c}_{1}}{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to \\pm \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more vertical as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/bf/bff6e7e5f4772441cdc81c809eadc377d3c2c43bd653d0c5c4f7f306adf79a5e.png)"] [17592186420024 :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2}\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}<0.$\n\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}<0.$\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies ${\\lambda }_{1}<0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}<0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-4x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-7y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$"] [17592186420025 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=3$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/29/29fba4c8d3fa024689c550da1bf54a50a81d73114ea1b2fa3a566966e9cb0692.png)"] [17592186420026 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-5$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/df/df61bf017441f2dc3e34253d89f98f42cce2ec1b27131b9204952b059e335af8.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-5t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}=\\frac{-5{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}}{-3{c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}}=\\frac{5{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/11/11484704b873e4f4cb449e8eb452e0e4e2ceb71d9f2918b052d5f18cbdd7ca7b.png)"] [17592186420413 :question/problem "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by $y=1-{x}^{2}$ and $y=0.$ Express $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{C}({x}^{2}-y)\\,dx+(2x-{y}^{2})\\,dy\n$$ as a repeated integral."] [17592186420849 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$"] [17592186420850 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(A).$"] [17592186420851 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}300 & 250 & 100 \\\\ 300 & 270 & 100 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [17592186420852 :question/problem "Find\n$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 114 & 210 & 198 \\\\ 113 & 210 & 198\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [17592186420854 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -6 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ find $det(C).$"] [17592186420954 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle D(y)=xy\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y,\n$$ then $D({x}^{2}-3x)=$"] [17592186420955 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle D=3{x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}-2,\n$$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=x-5?$"] [17592186421456 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle T=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -10 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=$"] [17592186422153 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)+3y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=5y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,\\text{ }0]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{5t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/2a/2ab8c69672acf64e93615960fdbb34445d4f81617cae16f90a8e4f30252607ba.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/d1/d1b7ffd22ad77fe94a994659fb7a091692b23bafb3012a705e964419fb71e8aa.png)\n\nFinally, since the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-9}},\nsolutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector\n{{field-10}} \nas they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7a/7a87773278ef8c921a9e7002a376bac1de99e969c2eb10e4a6f104a854094469.png)"] [17592186422153 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{5t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/2a/2ab8c69672acf64e93615960fdbb34445d4f81617cae16f90a8e4f30252607ba.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/d1/d1b7ffd22ad77fe94a994659fb7a091692b23bafb3012a705e964419fb71e8aa.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0<{\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}\n$$\n\nthus, ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/7a/7a87773278ef8c921a9e7002a376bac1de99e969c2eb10e4a6f104a854094469.png)"] [17592186422154 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)"] [17592186422154 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\n Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line.\n\n Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)"] [17592186422155 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-3x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f2/f20c6478af528b45ab2ea7dfded3e835c49a79178e11d7dd06bb13760874bbdb.png)"] [17592186422156 :question/problem "![](images/45/450d43a4cbf6dad8d54edbb66fc4a6e44e491290e1dc5d1303057fb0eb7928a7.png)\n\nThe phase portrait for a system of linear differential equations whose matrix has two real, distinct eigenvalues is shown above.\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[-1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[-1,\\text{ }2]^{T},$ respectively. \nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-2}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach \n{{field-3}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-4}}.\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-5}}.\n$$\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing \n{{field-6}},\nmeaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach \n{{field-7}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have \n{{field-8}}.\n\nFinally, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector {{field-9}} as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-10}}.\n\nTherefore,\n{{field-11}} \n$>0.$"] [17592186422156 :question/worked-solution "The straight-line solutions of the system have the form\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2dace7c4f02fa0575d70264f33805d15470fa0894622c4d26d80fc1221aa2c54.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{1}>0.$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x,\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/40/40f0bf749448807b1a3aa2b593ae2e30acf26fabedbdc45da75e338e84c328f2.png)\n\nThe tangents along this line are pointing $\\text{away from the origin},$ meaning that the solutions of the form \n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nmust approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we must have ${\\lambda }_{2}>0.$\n\nSo, for this particular system, both eigenvalues are positive. The dominant eigenvalue is the one with the greatest magnitude.\n\nNow, since the solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{2}$ as they move away from the origin, the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{2}.$\n\nTherefore, ${\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{1}>0.$"] [17592186423033 :question/problem "Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (2{cos}^{2}⁡x-1)\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [17592186423083 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$” is true. Indeed, for $x\\le 0,$ we have $f(x)=3{e}^{3x-1}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{e}^{3x-1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={e}^{3x-1}\\,{|}_{-\\infty }^{0} \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is false. Since statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\le 0$” only."] [17592186423084 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$” is true. Indeed, for $x\\ge 0,$ we have $f(x)={e}^{-x}\\ge 0.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =-(0-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$” and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ are true, the first and second conditions are satisfied. So, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\ge 0$”; $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1;\n$$ “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."] [17592186423131 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n$$\n\\displaystyle {a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$"] [17592186423157 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(7,4,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z+1}{-2}.\n$$"] [17592186423361 :question/worked-solution "We are given that \n\n$\\,$ $$\n\\displaystyle x=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\,y=\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the bilinear expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(x,y) & ={x}_{1}{y}_{1}-2{x}_{1}{y}_{2}+{x}_{1}{y}_{3}-{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{1}. \\\\  & =0\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 0\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0-0+0-1-2 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186423496 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/03dae0fbacc0339613580414c2217652b9ff6303788c7ca265deb3b1f755f4cd.png)"] [17592186423500 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u=6$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=6$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $6$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the fourth quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{14\\pi }{9}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6052c625de5a1d392975afae8c1053b2b430859ba6fbc3bc8c2c3de01d40318.png)"] [17592186423501 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49aa7d6ccd4b14b48ede7486f41ab11ac22b593c06d75795fe7819889b2b2f23.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement “The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis” is false, while statement “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis” is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement “The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1$ centered at $O.$” is true.\n\n- When $u=\\frac{1}{4},$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle (\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$$\n\\displaystyle (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\right)),\n$$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The image makes the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis”; “The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1$ centered at $O.$” only."] [17592186423502 :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\right),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\right)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}.\n$$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/50/50462754e89259447fe464f3a9c8ca7f165260d4b7adfc25585912291091838b.png)"] [17592186424097 :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{3}.$ Calculate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is any smooth path from $(-4,1)$ to $(0,-2)$ in ${R}^{2}.$"] [17592186424291 :question/problem "Identify the surface $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+{z}^{2}=-1.\n$$"] [17592186424327 :question/problem "![](images/a5/a56bcec93b5cc66ef8238b7fe7b213c5e184f1cf4b11baf4524525a63a284780.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where the region $D$ is enclosed between two circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above."] [17592186424328 :question/problem "![](images/3d/3d5542933934102149c58153c3108d55d9c615e991db8aca8d8fc1e60837917f.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ and the line $y=3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186424329 :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a51719e208dafaa32d7f28c338885313942513493bd9fe0392b1ef06004e91e.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is enclosed between the curve $(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the line $x=1,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,dA\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186424330 :question/problem "![](images/44/44b6b5826b5d8532fef3a9c8e56a03b3244a4aaa33ae00b4b12b69364c51f001.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the right-half plane is enclosed between two semi-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $4,$ as shown above. What is \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186424331 :question/problem "![](images/31/31054ef4614814dd1c9131192504ea37f3527af75d8292fbce943c16235c9303.png)\n\nThe region $D$ in the second quadrant is enclosed between two quarter-circles centered at $O$ of radii $1$ and $3,$ as shown above. What is\n$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ \nexpressed in polar coordinates?"] [17592186424594 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$"] [17592186424594 :question/worked-solution "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -10 & 15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\})=2.\n$$"] [17592186424595 :question/problem "Determine the dimension of $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$"] [17592186424620 :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3c874f3c4564795a2ddcfb4c2896c1bc0ef1b4b7b3d8e8318f42d15643942d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $UVWU,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?"] [17592186424621 :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d4c2c86ed8506ff034141ac20a5a56587a80370384ceeaf08c66f2ce3e8a315.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(4,-2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(4,-2)$ to the point $B(4,0).$\n\nGiven that $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{8}{3},\n$$ evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$"] [17592186424622 :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)={e}^{y+z}\\,i+{e}^{x+z}\\,j+{e}^{x+y}\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?\n$$"] [17592186424992 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [17592186424993 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186425335 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=7$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{7t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{7t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/71/71b5e685e876b0e7e33a3db5e1edc3579730d179867fe3b17b05574942201181.png)"] [17592186425336 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}={e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nNotice that for each vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},$ we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/bf/bffb2f815cc5a7db0c521054006aa834b17071f5dd33a97fe977e5d118b771be.png)"] [17592186425337 :question/problem "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=-x(t)+3y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvector $v=[1,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ corresponds to the repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of the system's matrix $A.$ The vector $w=[0,\\text{ }1]^{T}$ is such that $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{{field-1}}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}{{field-2}}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}} \nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}}  \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &={{field-6}} \\\\ y &=2(t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.37$ | $0$ | {{field-7}} |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $2$ | {{field-8}} |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/42/4232a3293f2414f6121c834fa09145f5f3b573d83ad7a3695c6ca93c228c8563.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e78fbd54f2825d28581b8181cab7762b7a625f7b21620eec37436771c9e1695.png)"] [17592186425337 :question/worked-solution "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\lambda =2,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{2t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{1}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/c9/c993f6fc9eb12c0d8f60c420519a7db4a5cf78da9f0c24d0ade87b0a96590f59.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\n\\displaystyle {c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhen ${c}_{2}=2,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t{e}^{2t} \\\\ y &=2(t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.37$ | $0$ | $0.60$ |\n| $y$ | $0.37$ | $2$ | $3.58$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/42/4232a3293f2414f6121c834fa09145f5f3b573d83ad7a3695c6ca93c228c8563.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/9e/9e78fbd54f2825d28581b8181cab7762b7a625f7b21620eec37436771c9e1695.png)"] [17592186425352 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 8 & -9 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [17592186425404 :question/problem "Given $$\n\\displaystyle G=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -15 & -12 & -16\\end{bmatrix},\\,\n$$ what is $\\text{dim}(\\text{Null}(G))?$"] [17592186425546 :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the binomial random variable $X∼B(6,0.48)$ given below, compute $P(X=3).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0198\\,$ | $\\,0.1293\\,$ | $\\,0.3820\\,$ | $\\,0.6930\\,$ | $\\,0.9083\\,$ | $\\,0.9878\\,$ | $\\,1.0000\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle P(X=3)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186425877 :question/problem "$$\n\\displaystyle |\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}|=\n$$"] [17592186425929 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](images/ce/ce9b9f3435a94b3f9f87b195e5b1591c80281b23cfb76ceb8e79cdeec3ae66b0.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}.\n$$ Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,2)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$ equals $x{e}^{y}$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ x{e}^{y}, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$ only."] [17592186425930 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,1]\\times [-3,2]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [-3,3]$” only."] [17592186425931 :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](images/3b/3bfc135232008824ae0522bcc8454c5f97c2dfd13fb6e333e146a040ccbc1f22.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+3y), & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given statements.\n\n- Statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [-1,3]$” is false. The rectangle $R$ does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Statements “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,2]$” is true. The rectangle $R$ covers the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Statement “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-3,3]\\times [-4,4]$” is false. The integrals have not been stated correctly with respect to the domains $R$ and $D$ (they have been swapped).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+3y)\\,\\text{d}A\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle ={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ $\\,$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,2]$” only."] [17592186426010 :question/problem "Given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{4}{7}\\times 5=\\frac{\\,\\boxed{}}{7},\n$$ the missing number is{{field-1}}."] [17592186426072 :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{5} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=5=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has no inverse.* $\\,✓$ \n\nIndeed, since $det(A)=0,$ the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $\\text{Null}(A)$ has dimension zero.* $\\,\\times$\n\nSince $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Null}(A)\\ne \\{0\\}$ which, in turn,  means that $\\text{Null}(A)$ must have a basis so the dimension must be greater than zero.\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n*If $A$ is a $5\\times 5$ matrix and $det(A)=0,$ then $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.* $\\,✓$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{dim(Col}(A))\\ne 5,$ which means that $A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ has no inverse”; “$A$ has less than $5$ pivot columns” only."] [17592186426076 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement I is true. Since $v\\ne 0$ and $Av=0,$ \nthen $v\\in \\text{Null}(A)$ and, therefore, $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186426077 :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$ is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is invertible” is false. Since $\\text{dim(Null}(A))\\ne 0,$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(A)<4$ is true. Indeed, since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\text{rank}(A)\\ne 4,$ or equivalently $\\text{rank}(A)<4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{dim(Null}(A))=1$; $\\text{rank}(A)<4$ only."] [17592186426267 :question/problem "A stick of length $1$ is broken at a point chosen uniformly at random along its length. Let the continuous random variable $X$ denote the distance from the left end of the stick to the break point, and let $Y=Y(X)$ denote the length of the longest piece.\n\nExpressing the integrands in the following equation as polynomials, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{E}[Y]={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{d}x+{\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\n$$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\text{d}x.$\n\nTherefore, the expected length of the longer piece is \n{{field-3}}."] [17592186426308 :question/problem "The entry ${a}_{21}^{′}$ in the inverse of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & k & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nequals $2.$ Find the value of $k.$"] [17592186426470 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186426471 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle L=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{5}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186426472 :question/problem "Find all real eigenvalues of the matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186426473 :question/problem "Find the characteristic equation of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"]}
